  <h2>Why people look for Node-RED alternatives, and what Flow keeps</h2>
  <p>Flow reads Node-RED's v1 <code>flows.json</code>, the flat JSON array behind every Node-RED export and flow library entry. Point it at your file and it runs. A node type this build has never heard of survives a load and save with every property intact, so you can run a flow here and hand it back to Node-RED afterward.</p>
  <p>We ran Node-RED's own 62 recorded example flows through both runtimes, and Flow gives what Node-RED gives on all 62. Anything that will not run is listed before you deploy, so the surprise happens at your desk and not on the line.</p>
  <p><a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/flow/migrating-from-node-red/">Read the migration guide</a> or <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/flow/compatibility/">check every node in the compatibility matrix</a>.</p>

  <h2>What changes when the plant depends on it</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>You get</th><th>What it means on the floor</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A ceiling on every queue</td><td>Every inbox is bounded with a policy per node: block, drop the newest, drop the oldest, or raise it to a Catch node. A chatty sensor and a slow database cannot run the box out of memory.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A thread for every node</td><td>A CPU-heavy Function node costs you a core, not the editor and every other flow on the box.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Every deploy is a record</td><td>Who, when, why, and the flow file byte for byte. A diff reads node by node and rollback redeploys an old record. "Who changed line 3 and put it back" is a command, not an archaeology project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tracing and replay</td><td>Click a message and the path it took lights up, with timing and payload at every hop. Replay it into a draft, or turn the trace into a test.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Durable wires</td><td>Mark a wire durable and messages go to disk in order when the node behind it stops answering, then drain when it returns. We killed a real InfluxDB for ten minutes and no rows went missing or doubled.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A sealed Function node</td><td>Function code runs with no access to the host, its files, or the network. Plugins are signed, sandboxed files, and an unsigned one refuses to load.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Locked down by default</td><td>Flow will not start without a login in front of it, and two-factor sign-in is free.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>It talks to the plant out of the box</h2>
  <p>Modbus TCP, OPC UA read, subscribe and write, S7 and EtherNet/IP, MQTT 5 with properties and the last will, TLS, and Sparkplug B as an edge node and as a host. The S7 and EtherNet/IP nodes were tested on bare metal. A new controller is still a commissioning job, so check one value you already know.</p>

  <h2>One small program</h2>
  <p>Flow is a 41.7 MB self-contained program that idles at about 16 MiB and answers its first request in 0.05 seconds. It runs on amd64 and arm64, there is a chart for Kubernetes, and upgrading is a plain image bump. <a href="/flow/#head-to-head">See the head-to-head against Node-RED</a>, same flow file on both runtimes.</p>
